Theologian Fleming Rutledge kind of, she has in her book,

00:43:40.760 --> 00:43:43.760
I've mentioned her a few times in her wonderful book, The Crucifixion.

00:43:43.760 --> 00:43:49.760
She kind of goes to bat for the blood, and she says, like, well, just keep in mind, it's poetry.

00:43:49.760 --> 00:43:51.760
That's her approach, and I think she's right.

00:43:51.760 --> 00:43:54.760
Like, it's not, I don't, I think we can take it overly literally, you know,

00:43:54.760 --> 00:43:57.760
like as though Christians literally, like, yes, Lord, I hope I get to heaven

00:43:57.760 --> 00:44:00.760
and I'm just bathed in the blood.

00:44:00.760 --> 00:44:04.760
It's like, what on earth? No, no, it's poetry.

00:44:04.760 --> 00:44:07.760
When we think of, what is blood a symbol of?

00:44:07.760 --> 00:44:09.760
It's like the essence of life.

00:44:09.760 --> 00:44:12.760
You know, so when we're saying, God, wash me in the blood,

00:44:12.760 --> 00:44:18.760
or write my name with the blood, you know, it's this kind of, this motif.

00:44:18.760 --> 00:44:23.760
So that's just another example, like another way of thinking about the cross.

00:44:23.760 --> 00:44:26.760
But the one, none of that's what I'm preaching on tonight.

00:44:26.760 --> 00:44:28.760
Long pramble, I better get going.

00:44:28.760 --> 00:44:30.760
We're going to be here all night.

00:44:30.760 --> 00:44:31.760
Long pramble, I know.

00:44:31.760 --> 00:44:33.760
See, the teaching hat goes on, and we're just like,

00:44:33.760 --> 00:44:35.760
oh, we're a little bit everywhere.

00:44:35.760 --> 00:44:38.760
So what I want to talk about tonight is the way that Christ,

00:44:38.760 --> 00:44:42.760
this language, how his life has been given for us.

00:44:42.760 --> 00:44:46.760
And this leads us to the notion of the substitute.

00:44:46.760 --> 00:44:52.760
Where, well, before I jump into the details, I'll just name,

00:44:52.760 --> 00:44:56.760
this is probably the most, I'd say, controversial in modern times

00:44:56.760 --> 00:44:58.760
kind of approach to the cross.

00:44:58.760 --> 00:44:59.760
It's interesting.

00:44:59.760 --> 00:45:01.760
It's very polarized.

00:45:01.760 --> 00:45:03.760
Yeah, it's very polarizing.

00:45:03.760 --> 00:45:07.760
For many in kind of more, I should say, conservative Christian circles,

00:45:07.760 --> 00:45:11.760
this isn't just like one way among others to think of the cross.

00:45:11.760 --> 00:45:16.760
This is the way to think of the cross.

00:45:16.760 --> 00:45:21.760
And then you have kind of in more progressive Christian circles,

00:45:21.760 --> 00:45:25.760
this is like the terrible way to think of the cross.

00:45:26.760 --> 00:45:29.760
In ways, it becomes sort of a line in the sand.

00:45:29.760 --> 00:45:33.760
So how do I know if I'm like a more conservative kind of theological Christian

00:45:33.760 --> 00:45:34.760
or like a progressive?

00:45:34.760 --> 00:45:36.760
And a lot of times it's this.

00:45:36.760 --> 00:45:39.760
Like, well, do you believe in what's known as substitutionary atonement?

00:45:39.760 --> 00:45:43.760
Do you believe Christ was the substitute dying for you?

00:45:43.760 --> 00:45:46.760
And that's often where in people it gets very polarized.

00:45:46.760 --> 00:45:49.760
Now, I know some of you I see heads nodding like you're in the loop.

00:45:49.760 --> 00:45:51.760
Others are kind of like, I had no idea.

00:45:51.760 --> 00:45:52.760
And that's fine.

00:45:53.760 --> 00:45:56.760
If you're into theology and you read Christian blogs,

00:45:56.760 --> 00:46:01.760
well then, oh my goodness, you'd be all into this topic.

00:46:01.760 --> 00:46:04.760
It's an interesting one.

00:46:04.760 --> 00:46:06.760
So tonight, I guess kind of where I want to take it,

00:46:06.760 --> 00:46:12.760
I'm going to draw a lot from Fleming Rutledge for this one, this message.

00:46:12.760 --> 00:46:15.760
I think she does a good job of helping us think this through.

00:46:15.760 --> 00:46:19.760
And she kind of pulls from the good parts of everyone

00:46:19.760 --> 00:46:21.760
and has some criticisms, critiques.

00:46:21.760 --> 00:46:24.760
So I think she did a really good job of at least helping me think it through.

00:46:24.760 --> 00:46:27.760
So hopefully, I can in turn do this for you.

00:46:27.760 --> 00:46:28.760
So, all right.

00:46:28.760 --> 00:46:29.760
So where are we?

00:46:29.760 --> 00:46:31.760
What's up with this idea of Christ the substitute?

00:46:31.760 --> 00:46:32.760
Where does it come from?

00:46:32.760 --> 00:46:33.760
What's going on here?

00:46:33.760 --> 00:46:38.760
A lot of the controversy stems from one Greek word, huper.

00:46:38.760 --> 00:46:39.760
It's funny.

00:46:39.760 --> 00:46:42.760
Isn't it how like one word can just send people in different directions

00:46:42.760 --> 00:46:46.760
and like split churches and, you know, it's remarkable.

00:46:46.760 --> 00:46:51.760
So the word huper, it means for, on behalf of, in place of.

00:46:51.760 --> 00:46:55.760
You see this scattered all throughout the scriptures, both Old and New Testaments.

00:46:55.760 --> 00:46:57.760
I'll give you a few examples.

00:46:57.760 --> 00:47:02.760
Galatians 3.13, Christ redeemed us from the curse of the law

00:47:02.760 --> 00:47:08.760
by becoming a curse for us, huper.

00:47:08.760 --> 00:47:12.760
So you can see the language of the curse and then he becomes the curse,

00:47:12.760 --> 00:47:14.760
takes on the curse for us.

00:47:14.760 --> 00:47:17.760
The word is written, cursed is everyone who's hung on a pole.

00:47:17.760 --> 00:47:19.760
Other translations say a tree.

00:47:19.760 --> 00:47:20.760
Or 1 Peter 3.18.

00:47:20.760 --> 00:47:24.760
This is actually, we touched on this last week.

00:47:24.760 --> 00:47:31.760
For Christ also suffered once for sins, the righteous for the unrighteous.

00:47:31.760 --> 00:47:32.760
So there it is again, right?

00:47:32.760 --> 00:47:38.760
So Christ, the righteous one, suffering, dying for, on behalf of, or in place of.

00:47:38.760 --> 00:47:42.760
This is where translators kind of have to make some decisions on how do they,

00:47:42.760 --> 00:47:44.760
as you can see, they keep opting just for the word for,

00:47:44.760 --> 00:47:46.760
which just leaves it a little more vague.

00:47:46.760 --> 00:47:48.760
But it's that word, huper.

00:47:48.760 --> 00:47:50.760
For the righteous, for the unrighteous, to bring you to God.

00:47:50.760 --> 00:47:53.760
He was put to death in the body, but made alive in the spirit.

00:47:53.760 --> 00:47:57.760
Here's from Isaiah 53, verses 5 through 6.

00:47:57.760 --> 00:48:02.760
And this, obviously it's not the Greek word, because this is like Old Testament,

00:48:02.760 --> 00:48:04.760
so written in Hebrew, different word.

00:48:04.760 --> 00:48:06.760
But you can kind of hear the idea kind of rumbling around.

00:48:06.760 --> 00:48:11.760
It says this, but he was pierced for, whose transgressions?

00:48:11.760 --> 00:48:14.760
For our transgressions.

00:48:14.760 --> 00:48:17.760
He was crushed for our iniquities.

00:48:17.760 --> 00:48:21.760
The punishment that brought us peace was on him.

00:48:21.760 --> 00:48:23.760
By his wounds we are healed.

00:48:23.760 --> 00:48:25.760
We all, like sheep, have gone astray.

00:48:25.760 --> 00:48:32.760
Each of us has turned to his own way, and the Lord has laid on him the iniquity of us all.

00:48:32.760 --> 00:48:38.760
So you can see where this idea, where we get this idea of Christ as dying,

00:48:38.760 --> 00:48:43.760
dying in some sense for us, on our behalf, in our place, as our substitute.

00:48:43.760 --> 00:48:48.760
In just a moment, I'm going to get to some of the objections to this.

00:48:48.760 --> 00:48:53.760
And you can maybe already see where what people start getting very nervous about

00:48:53.760 --> 00:48:57.760
is sort of the image of God hovering in the background of this.

00:48:57.760 --> 00:49:00.760
Because it's like, well, if that terrible thing happened to Jesus,

00:49:00.760 --> 00:49:05.760
is the idea like, God wanted that terrible thing to happen to you.

00:49:05.760 --> 00:49:09.760
But then he's like, okay, no, instead I'll punish my son.

00:49:09.760 --> 00:49:15.760
So it's kind of here where people start getting nervous with this notion.

00:49:15.760 --> 00:49:21.760
So I'm going to give you tonight three objections and then three sort of responses.

00:49:21.760 --> 00:49:23.760
So if you're a note-taker, you can jot these down.

00:49:23.760 --> 00:49:28.760
Objection number one, notions of Christ's substitution don't make sense

00:49:28.760 --> 00:49:33.760
because an innocent person cannot take on the guilt of another.

00:49:33.760 --> 00:49:35.760
That should be one kind of critique.

00:49:35.760 --> 00:49:39.760
If you've ever been in church circles, you've heard maybe a preacher,

00:49:39.760 --> 00:49:43.760
when they're preaching the gospel, they might give an example like this.

00:49:43.760 --> 00:49:50.760
Imagine that you went to trial for a crime.

00:49:50.760 --> 00:49:52.760
And it was a crime, indeed, you were guilty of.

00:49:52.760 --> 00:49:55.760
And you go through the whole trial, and the jury goes back, they deliberate,

00:49:55.760 --> 00:49:59.760
they come back, and the judge says, okay, what did you find?

00:49:59.760 --> 00:50:04.760
And the jury announces you guilty.

00:50:04.760 --> 00:50:09.760
And, in fact, they go even so far as to punish you to death.

00:50:09.760 --> 00:50:12.760
But it's then, this is where preachers love this kind of stuff,

00:50:12.760 --> 00:50:19.760
it's then that the judge stands up and says, no,

00:50:19.760 --> 00:50:25.760
I will take the punishment on their behalf, in their place.

00:50:25.760 --> 00:50:30.760
So that would be, and this is what Christ does,

00:50:30.760 --> 00:50:34.760
steps in, the judge judged in our place.

00:50:34.760 --> 00:50:39.760
Now, where this objection kind of pushes back is it says,

00:50:39.760 --> 00:50:41.760
well, see, the only problem is that doesn't make any sense

00:50:41.760 --> 00:50:43.760
because you can't do that.

00:50:43.760 --> 00:50:45.760
Like a judge can't literally stand up and be like, hey, you know what?

00:50:45.760 --> 00:50:47.760
I'm taking this one, I'm going to go to jail,

00:50:47.760 --> 00:50:51.760
or I'm going to go to the electric chair, whatever, on your behalf, in your place.

00:50:51.760 --> 00:50:53.760
They're like, well, you can't do that.

00:50:53.760 --> 00:50:56.760
Christians cannot take on the guilt of other people.

00:50:56.760 --> 00:50:59.760
So this is sort of one objection.

00:50:59.760 --> 00:51:02.760
It's like, what, you can't take on guilt?

00:51:02.760 --> 00:51:04.760
Like, no, my guilt's my own.

00:51:04.760 --> 00:51:06.760
That's kind of the objection.

00:51:06.760 --> 00:51:07.760
All right, second objection.

00:51:07.760 --> 00:51:12.760
Notions of Christ's substitution are divine child abuse.

00:51:12.760 --> 00:51:20.760
This one's, this is pretty, I'd say more powerful a critique than the first one.

00:51:20.760 --> 00:51:22.760
So, okay, what does it mean?

00:51:22.760 --> 00:51:25.760
Imagine like a very different revival meeting.

00:51:25.760 --> 00:51:27.760
And here the preacher, you know, preaching the gospel,

00:51:27.760 --> 00:51:34.760
and so they say, imagine that you sinned,

00:51:34.760 --> 00:51:42.760
and your father became very, very angry.

00:51:42.760 --> 00:51:49.760
So angry that his wrath was going to be poured out upon you.

00:51:49.760 --> 00:51:57.760
But it's then that your noble older brother steps up and says,

00:51:57.760 --> 00:52:01.760
no, dad, don't do it.

00:52:01.760 --> 00:52:03.760
Hurt me instead.

00:52:03.760 --> 00:52:09.760
I'll take the punishment so that they don't have to.

00:52:09.760 --> 00:52:14.760
And then the preacher says, see, this, this is what Christ has done.

00:52:14.760 --> 00:52:20.760
He has absorbed the wrath of the Father so that you wouldn't have to.

00:52:20.760 --> 00:52:23.760
He's gone to the cross so that you don't have to.

00:52:23.760 --> 00:52:26.760
And that's the good news of the gospel.

00:52:26.760 --> 00:52:28.760
All right.

00:52:28.760 --> 00:52:30.760
Can you hear where this starts to get problematic?

00:52:30.760 --> 00:52:32.760
Like in kind of where people are getting real nervous.

00:52:32.760 --> 00:52:34.760
Like, oh, gosh.

00:52:34.760 --> 00:52:36.760
Again, you can kind of hear the image of God.

00:52:36.760 --> 00:52:41.760
You can see it kind of happening there, you know.

00:52:41.760 --> 00:52:45.760
And, in fact, this really we're coming back to the language of abuse.

00:52:45.760 --> 00:52:50.760
You know, this gets problematic where this is how actually abusive households,

00:52:50.760 --> 00:52:52.760
it can kind of play out is something like this,

00:52:52.760 --> 00:52:54.760
where you might have like one spouse, you know,

00:52:54.760 --> 00:52:59.760
like who is going to kind of distract the abusive one so they don't hurt the kids.

00:52:59.760 --> 00:53:04.760
Or maybe there is like one of the kids has kind of learned how to like distract,

00:53:04.760 --> 00:53:09.760
you know, drunk mom or dad to kind of take the abuse.

00:53:09.760 --> 00:53:15.760
You know, so when you kind of start using this metaphor and then like, you know,

00:53:15.760 --> 00:53:20.760
preaching it and so this is like this is where just people don't like this notion of,

00:53:20.760 --> 00:53:23.760
you know, Christ as substitute.

00:53:23.760 --> 00:53:24.760
All right.

00:53:24.760 --> 00:53:25.760
Third objection.

00:53:25.760 --> 00:53:30.760
Notions of Christ's substitution depict a vindictive God.

00:53:30.760 --> 00:53:36.760
I think this is more making just naming clearly what the other two are already implying.

00:53:36.760 --> 00:53:40.760
But Rutledge, he quotes a French writer, Antoine Vergote.

00:53:40.760 --> 00:53:42.760
I don't know how to, I don't speak French.

00:53:42.760 --> 00:53:45.760
Vergote, I have no idea.

00:53:45.760 --> 00:53:48.760
He said this, Antoine said this,

00:53:48.760 --> 00:53:54.760
can one imagine a more obsessional phantasm than that of a God who demands the torturing

00:53:54.760 --> 00:53:59.760
of his own son to death as a satisfaction for his anger.

00:53:59.760 --> 00:54:04.760
So you can hear where the critiques are coming from.

00:54:04.760 --> 00:54:07.760
Playing around.

00:54:07.760 --> 00:54:09.760
Yeah, these are, I think, some really powerful critiques.

00:54:09.760 --> 00:54:11.760
So, okay, so what do we make of this?

00:54:11.760 --> 00:54:14.760
On the one hand, you have kind of the scriptures are, you know,

00:54:14.760 --> 00:54:19.760
I think it would be really challenging to argue that scriptures never are talking about

00:54:19.760 --> 00:54:24.760
something like Christ the substitute.

00:54:24.760 --> 00:54:26.760
But on the other hand, you have these objections, you know.

00:54:26.760 --> 00:54:28.760
So I'm going to offer a few responses.

00:54:28.760 --> 00:54:30.760
First, response number one.

00:54:30.760 --> 00:54:33.760
Context is king.

00:54:33.760 --> 00:54:35.760
Context is king.

00:54:35.760 --> 00:54:41.760
And, you know, for example, I do think there would be something like really messed up,

00:54:41.760 --> 00:54:45.760
in particular, with us, I don't know,

00:54:45.760 --> 00:54:49.760
like I'm imagining someone who's teaching this kiddos in this older kids classroom

00:54:49.760 --> 00:54:52.760
right over here right now, you know, standing up and saying,

00:54:52.760 --> 00:54:56.760
kids, you know, have you ever lied to your parents?

00:54:56.760 --> 00:54:58.760
You know, they're like, yeah.

00:54:58.760 --> 00:55:01.760
Have you ever taken someone's toy?

00:55:01.760 --> 00:55:02.760
Yeah.

00:55:02.760 --> 00:55:06.760
Well, then you deserve the cross, you know.

00:55:06.760 --> 00:55:09.760
But the good news is that Jesus went to the cross for it.

00:55:09.760 --> 00:55:13.760
You can see where it's just so, like, kind of extreme, and, you know, it's just like,

00:55:13.760 --> 00:55:19.760
I, the punishment doesn't fit the crime, I don't know.

00:55:19.760 --> 00:55:21.760
And I would agree.

00:55:21.760 --> 00:55:26.760
I think, yeah, this can become, like, sort of psychologically damaging, even for kids and stuff.

00:55:26.760 --> 00:55:29.760
Like, sort of, you know, they get very scared of, like, Father God, you know,

00:55:29.760 --> 00:55:32.760
and it's, yeah, there's something to this.

00:55:32.760 --> 00:55:37.760
However, it was actually the psychologist and Christian writer Richard Beck,

00:55:37.760 --> 00:55:42.760
and we did a whole series on one of his books, Unclean, a few months back.

00:55:42.760 --> 00:55:45.760
And he has had quite an interesting journey with all this, with the cross,

00:55:45.760 --> 00:55:51.760
and he teaches a weekly Bible study to some inmates outside of,

00:55:51.760 --> 00:55:55.760
there's a prison outside, I think it's Abilene, where he lives.

00:55:55.760 --> 00:56:03.760
And he, for years, he leans definitely towards the more progressive kind of wing of the church.

00:56:03.760 --> 00:56:09.760
And so he really avoided, you know, Christ the substitute kind of stuff.

00:56:09.760 --> 00:56:11.760
Just was like, I'm not going to go there, you know.

00:56:11.760 --> 00:56:14.760
And so he did a lot of Christ the victor teaching

00:56:14.760 --> 00:56:18.760
and would lean towards that in his interpretations and such when he's teaching inmates.

00:56:18.760 --> 00:56:23.760
But one day, I think they were studying Isaiah 53, that passage we read earlier about, you know,

00:56:23.760 --> 00:56:25.760
the Lord has laid on him the iniquity of us all.

00:56:25.760 --> 00:56:27.760
We all, like sheep, have gone astray and, you know, all that.

00:56:27.760 --> 00:56:35.760
So kind of going through it, and he started to talk about this idea of kind of Christ the substitute

00:56:35.760 --> 00:56:39.760
because that passage just seemed to lend itself so much to it.

00:56:39.760 --> 00:56:46.760
And he could tell just from, like, their body language, the tears in some of their eyes,

00:56:46.760 --> 00:56:52.760
that it was beginning to resonate very, very powerfully with them.

00:56:53.760 --> 00:56:58.760
And as he started to dialogue, because, of course, it was a Bible study, so it's, like, discussion-based.

00:56:58.760 --> 00:57:02.760
As they started to talk, out came their stories.

00:57:02.760 --> 00:57:05.760
And what these—in this prison it was men.

00:57:05.760 --> 00:57:13.760
So what these men had done, things that they found simply unforgivable.

00:57:13.760 --> 00:57:19.760
And they were struggling so mightily with guilt and with shame.

00:57:20.760 --> 00:57:28.760
And so far from seeing in this a kind of, you know, oppressive thing,

00:57:28.760 --> 00:57:41.760
they found it a beautiful picture of the gospel, that Christ had taken on that guilt,

00:57:41.760 --> 00:57:46.760
had taken on that evil, had kind of absorbed it into his very being

00:57:46.760 --> 00:57:49.760
and then went to the cross for them.

00:57:49.760 --> 00:57:59.760
Even though it is—one way to think of it—it's a very, very strong metaphor, a strong picture.

00:57:59.760 --> 00:58:07.760
It's, like, very intense, which is why I'd be very hesitant to have our folks doing lots of kids teaching on this.

00:58:07.760 --> 00:58:09.760
Because that's where it's, like, a powerful metaphor.

00:58:09.760 --> 00:58:18.760
But you could hear, even in my message last week, where when—because I don't think it's just, you know, prisoners—

00:58:18.760 --> 00:58:23.760
we, too, at times, have done things that we feel are, like, unforgivable,

00:58:23.760 --> 00:58:25.760
things we wouldn't share with anyone.

00:58:25.760 --> 00:58:29.760
We don't, like—we're going to take it to our grave, kind of.

00:58:29.760 --> 00:58:31.760
And we feel it's unforgivable.

00:58:31.760 --> 00:58:39.760
And so, suddenly, this notion of Christ as our substitute, Christ dying for us, on behalf of us,

00:58:39.760 --> 00:58:50.760
taking the punishment that we deserved, can become incredibly, incredibly liberating and helpful.

00:58:50.760 --> 00:58:54.760
So, that's kind of the first response.

00:58:54.760 --> 00:58:56.760
Context is everything.

00:58:56.760 --> 00:59:01.760
You know, pre-K, Sunday school, class, is completely different than adults,

00:59:01.760 --> 00:59:08.760
who have done adult things at times, and can be struggling with guilt and such.

00:59:08.760 --> 00:59:10.760
Response number two.

00:59:10.760 --> 00:59:19.760
We need a poetic ear and a flexible theological imagination, not one that's overly logical and wooden.

00:59:19.760 --> 00:59:22.760
I'll just really quickly touch on this.

00:59:22.760 --> 00:59:27.760
This is kind of a similar point as with the blood of Christ, where we've got to remember,

00:59:27.760 --> 00:59:33.760
where God-talk is really challenging.

00:59:33.760 --> 00:59:43.760
At some level, we are always describing realities where, ultimately, language will begin to break down and fail us.

00:59:43.760 --> 00:59:45.760
I mean, if you ask me, like,

00:59:45.760 --> 00:59:53.760
well, Brett, just explain to me clearly, what was happening in the cross?

00:59:53.760 --> 00:59:54.760
Ultimately, what do I have?

00:59:54.760 --> 00:59:59.760
I have a bunch of stories and metaphors.

00:59:59.760 --> 01:00:01.760
I mean, even think of the way preachers preach it.

01:00:01.760 --> 01:00:03.760
Like, I was doing some of them terrible metaphors.

01:00:03.760 --> 01:00:07.760
But, you know, well, imagine it's like a judge, and there you are.

01:00:07.760 --> 01:00:08.760
You know what I mean?

01:00:08.760 --> 01:00:09.760
You can see where, what is that?

01:00:09.760 --> 01:00:12.760
Are you saying, oh, literally, there's going to be a trial?

01:00:12.760 --> 01:00:13.760
No, it's this.

01:00:13.760 --> 01:00:14.760
It's a parable.

01:00:14.760 --> 01:00:15.760
It's a story.

01:00:15.760 --> 01:00:18.760
It's a metaphor to try to get us thinking.

01:00:18.760 --> 01:00:19.760
You know what I mean?

01:00:19.760 --> 01:00:23.760
It's sort of the old adage about the finger pointing to the moon is not the moon itself.

01:00:23.760 --> 01:00:29.760
So all these ways of describing the cross are just trying to get at something quite unnameable.

01:00:29.760 --> 01:00:32.760
I mean, ultimately, it's beyond words.

01:00:32.760 --> 01:00:38.760
Whatever was happening in the cross, it's like words fail.

01:00:38.760 --> 01:00:44.760
So we've got to remember, we need a kind of flexible theological imagination.

01:00:44.760 --> 01:00:45.760
We need poetry.

01:00:45.760 --> 01:00:49.760
We need words that are pointers to the truth.

01:00:49.760 --> 01:00:57.760
While not getting overly hung up and literal-minded with these things.

01:00:57.760 --> 01:01:00.760
You know, for example, I think that critique about human guilt.

01:01:00.760 --> 01:01:01.760
Like, oh, this doesn't make sense.

01:01:01.760 --> 01:01:03.760
Humans can't take on the guilt of another.

01:01:03.760 --> 01:01:06.760
Like, that's where I think kind of the trap that that falls into.

01:01:07.760 --> 01:01:13.760
Like, wait, I'm really trying to make this make complete, logical, human-level sense.

01:01:13.760 --> 01:01:17.760
And it's sort of like, well, this is God we're talking about.

01:01:17.760 --> 01:01:20.760
And God is not just like a human.

01:01:20.760 --> 01:01:23.760
So I can't take on your guilt, but who's to say God couldn't?

01:01:23.760 --> 01:01:28.760
So that's where we kind of need that flexible theological imagination.

01:01:29.760 --> 01:01:31.760
Third response, and I'll close with this.

01:01:31.760 --> 01:01:36.760
Trinitarian theology is absolutely essential.

01:01:36.760 --> 01:01:38.760
And that's a big word, Trinitarian theology.

01:01:38.760 --> 01:01:45.760
It just means this notion of God as Father, Son, Spirit, as loving relationship itself.

01:01:45.760 --> 01:01:50.760
And you'll recall the second critique was about divine child abuse.

01:01:50.760 --> 01:01:56.760
And one of the problems, often how this has been preached in really dangerous, messed-up ways,

01:01:56.760 --> 01:02:05.760
is to make it sound like God the Father and God the Son are kind of, I don't know, against each other or something.

01:02:05.760 --> 01:02:07.760
You know, where it's like, God the Father's all angry.

01:02:07.760 --> 01:02:10.760
He's like, well, I need someone to vent my wrath on.

01:02:10.760 --> 01:02:13.760
And then Jesus is like, okay, I guess it'll be me.

01:02:13.760 --> 01:02:14.760
He's like, yeah, it'll be you.

01:02:14.760 --> 01:02:16.760
It gets so weird.

01:02:16.760 --> 01:02:22.760
And that's just a completely terrible metaphor in a way to think of the cross of Christ.

01:02:22.760 --> 01:02:25.760
What's happening in this notion of Christ as substitute?

01:02:25.760 --> 01:02:36.760
It's really the idea of God giving God's self on our behalf in our place.

01:02:36.760 --> 01:02:40.760
It's not like the Father and the Son kind of at odds.

01:02:40.760 --> 01:02:47.760
It's Jesus with one will, willing with one will what the Father wills.

01:02:47.760 --> 01:02:49.760
It's Christ.

01:02:49.760 --> 01:02:53.760
It's God the Father, Son, Spirit entering into the cross.

01:02:53.760 --> 01:02:57.760
And that's where there's a book called The Crucified God by Juergen Moltmann,

01:02:57.760 --> 01:03:00.760
which really pushes this idea to its extremes.

01:03:00.760 --> 01:03:05.760
Because some theologians have even tried to kind of parse it out like, well, no, it was only like God the Son.

01:03:05.760 --> 01:03:07.760
There's only a part of God that really went to the cross.

01:03:07.760 --> 01:03:13.760
And really, I think the truth is this is God giving God's self.

01:03:13.760 --> 01:03:15.760
And that changes.

01:03:15.760 --> 01:03:18.760
I think that completely changes the metaphor.

01:03:18.760 --> 01:03:22.760
It completely changes the way we think about this.

01:03:22.760 --> 01:03:24.760
The way I might sum it up is this.

01:03:24.760 --> 01:03:33.760
The notion of Christ as our substitute was not originally intended to be a picture of an angry, bloodthirsty deity venting his wrath upon his son.

01:03:33.760 --> 01:03:40.760
It was a picture of God's self-sacrificial love for us and all creation.

01:03:40.760 --> 01:03:45.760
I would say that's the heart of this.

01:03:45.760 --> 01:03:50.760
But at the end of the day, there are many ways to think of the cross.

01:03:51.760 --> 01:03:58.760
And so if you find yourself just in a place where you're just like, I can't do it, you have a place at the table.

01:03:58.760 --> 01:04:04.760
And if you're like, oh, man, I love this notion of Christ the substitute, you have a place at the table.

01:04:04.760 --> 01:04:14.760
And I think the church has been divided far too long on topics like this that the truth is we can come to the table as God's children,

01:04:14.760 --> 01:04:19.760
even in the midst of our agreements and our disagreements and everything in between.

01:04:19.760 --> 01:04:27.760
So I really look forward to hearing about your meetup discussions this week and how everyone's able to process all this.

01:04:27.760 --> 01:04:31.760
Because I do. I hope it'll foster some good conversation.

01:04:31.760 --> 01:04:40.760
So as we come to the table of the Lord tonight, as we take in the body of Christ in the bread, the blood of Christ in the wine,

01:04:40.760 --> 01:04:53.760
it's a moment where we get to connect afresh and anew with Jesus Christ who promised to be present to us in this moment.

01:04:53.760 --> 01:04:56.760
I mean, at some level, always, but in a really special way.

01:04:56.760 --> 01:05:00.760
There is something unique and powerful about this moment where we come to the table of the Lord.

01:05:00.760 --> 01:05:08.760
So I encourage you, if there's anything in your heart tonight where something in you wants to connect with Jesus,

01:05:08.760 --> 01:05:14.760
then I encourage you to to come forward and participate with us tonight.

